// @check
// @error no method '.charAt' on string; did you mean '.slice(i, i + 1)'?
// @error no method '.silce' on string; did you mean '.slice'?
// @error no method '.pusch' on array i32[]; did you mean '.push'?
// @error no method '.toUpperAscci' on string; did you mean '.toUpperAscii'?
// CONTRACT (must-error): the `no method` diagnostic ends by naming the method the author
// almost certainly wanted, from TWO sources, and the refusal itself is unchanged (D1599).
//
// The first line is the external consumer glean's report VL-045 verbatim — `s.charAt(i)`,
// the JS/Java/Python spelling a reader coming from almost any other language tries first.
// It reports the same shape of complaint as VL-039 (D1590, this diagnostic's `unknown
// type` sibling): the checker knows the fix and used to not say it.
//
//   1 A NEWCOMER MAP, keyed by receiver kind and exact/case-folded: `charAt` names a call
//     with DIFFERENT ARGUMENTS (`slice` takes a range, not an index), so the suggestion
//     shows the whole shape rather than a bare name.
//   2 An EDIT DISTANCE over the names in scope for that receiver — the intrinsics
//     (`silce` against `slice`, `pusch` against `push`) plus every imported or declared
//     self-function the receiver could dispatch to (`toUpperAscci` against the imported
//     `toUpperAscii`).
//
// The suffix is a SUFFIX: `no method '.charAt' on string` is byte-identical to what this
// diagnostic said before, and `no-method-no-near-name.vl` is the negative control beside
// it — a name with nothing near it keeps the bare text. `tests/vl_no_method_suggestion_test.ts`
// asserts both EXACTLY, which this tier cannot (an `@error` directive matches by substring).
import { toUpperAscii } from "std:str"

const s = "hello"
const i = 1
const xs = [1, 2, 3]

print(s.charAt(i))
print(s.silce(0, 1))
print(xs.pusch(4))
print(s.toUpperAscii())
print(s.toUpperAscci())
